Trigger `ta-anchor-dynamic-refresh__finding72-sequencing-constraint-scored.md` (`.WIP`). Finding 72
names no work for me and closes the proof chain, but its §4 re-states **frequency of the `deadRC > 0`
state** as still open — the one AD5 item that was mine. It is now answered, and the answer is worse
than "rare." **No code changed, nothing committed, nothing pushed.**

## 1. Finding 72 — accepted in full, including the narrowing that costs me something

All three items accepted. §2(ii) is right that I under-stated my own point: the `states == nil` branch
(`cost_aware_optimizer.go:144`) is dead **tree-wide**, not merely production-unreachable — no test
calls `scaleDownVariantSet` directly and all five `scaleDownRoleIterated` test callers pass a map
literal. §3's narrowing lands: the proof is **per-variant**, so "floor first, then tidy" is safe
*exactly for floored variants*, which makes the ordering constraint **necessary but not sufficient**
and does **not** rescue option (a) — its safe branch presumes complete floor coverage, which
operator-set configuration is what cannot guarantee. Recording the coverage precondition alongside
the ordering constraint, as asked, so the record cannot read as a licence to tidy up.

## 2. Frequency — the `deadRC > 0` state has an ordinary production route, and it is cold start

I had said my fixture carries no information about frequency and that only a hand-built ballot reaches
the state. **The second half was wrong.** Traced from the analyzer that produces the result:

**The dead end that makes it look safe.** Saturation's per-variant `TotalDemand` accumulates *only*
inside the `len(replicas) > 0` branch (`saturation_v2/analyzer.go:397`), so a variant labelled
`satReasonNoData` (`:430-432`) contributes **zero** demand. Read no further and "all variants NoData
⇒ no demand ⇒ RC 0" looks airtight.

**The second, independent demand source.** Role demand is not only the sum over variants:
`estimateSchedulerQueueDemand` (`:750-794`) is added per role *after* aggregation (`:487-492`). Its
input-token term is
`tokensFromBytes = QueueBytes / BytesPerToken` (`:763`) — which **reads no replica metrics at all**.
With no replica metrics, `computeModelWorkloadAverages` returns 0/0/0 (`:598-613`), so
`tokensFromCount` and `avgHitRate` both vanish and `inputTokens = QueueBytes / BytesPerToken`,
positive whenever `QueueBytes > 0`. Role attribution needs no metrics either —
`byRole[prefill] = inputTokens`, `byRole[decode] = inputTokens + outputTokens` (`:783-786`) — and
both `activeRoles` (`:104-107`) and `hasDisaggregation` (`:471-478`) are derived from
`VariantStates`, i.e. from the CR, not from telemetry.

**Liveness closes it.** `perAnalyzer[nr.Name]` is only ever written from an **informative** result, so
an analyzer that has never produced one has no entry at all and `ok == false` → `Live = false`
(`saturation/engine_v2.go:235-248`).

**Composite: first deploy of a P/D model with requests queued upstream and no priceable variant —
no ready replicas, no own capacity record, no compatible record via `lookupCompatibleCapacity` —
yields `Enabled && !Live` with positive `RequiredCapacity` on both roles.** That is the `deadRC > 0`
regime, reached by the most ordinary startup path there is, with no hand-built ballot.

**The precondition, stated tightly so nobody over-claims it:** it needs `SchedulerQueue.QueueBytes >
0` specifically. With `QueueSize > 0` but `QueueBytes == 0` and no replica metrics, both input-token
terms are zero and the route does not open. This is a **reachability** answer, not a rate — how often
real clusters sit there needs production data, not source.

## 3. What happens there, measured — prefill **freezes**, it does not drain to 1

Built the cold-start shape (dead saturation carrying 470.588 on both roles, live throughput carrying
decode 4000 and prefill's structural zero, P/D variants at `cur` replicas) and ran both optimizers at
HEAD and at base `075a208e`:

| `cur` | base pf | base dc | HEAD pf | HEAD dc |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 1 | 4 | **0** | 4 |
| 1 | 2 | 5 | **1** | 5 |
| 2 | 3 | 6 | **2** | 6 |

Both optimizers identical in every row; decode identical across columns (the dominance combine lets
4000 swamp 470.588).

**The mechanism is not the one AD5 is written around.** With decode's RC positive,
`anyRoleNeedsScaleUp` is **true** (printed, not inferred), so the dispatch takes the scale-up branch
(`cost_aware_optimizer.go:59-66`) and `scaleDownRoleIterated` is **never entered** — the cheapest-at-1
accident is not involved on this path at all. Prefill gets nothing because its RC is 0, so it stays
where it is.

**So AD5 has two regimes, with different mechanisms and different brakes:**

- **(i) decode RC > 0** — rising load, cold start. Scale-up branch. Prefill **frozen at current**;
  no brake exists or is needed. At `cur = 0` that means prefill stays at **0** while decode scales to
  4 — a P/D model with no prefill replicas. The enforcer does not rescue it: its minimum-replica
  preservation fires only when the model's *total* is zero (`enforcer.go:140-180`), and the total here
  is 4.
- **(ii) decode RC == 0 with spare** — steady/falling load. Scale-down branch. Prefill **drains to
  1**, and the cheapest-at-1 accident is the only brake.

The Type 1's *"prefill's target collapses to 1"* describes **(ii)** only. Left unqualified it
understates (i), where there is no floor to collapse onto.

**Attribution unchanged, and now numbered.** Same regime-split as before — this is the `deadRC > 0`
half, so **HEAD-only**, opened by `VG-up` (mine): base keeps the dead entry on the ballot and converts
its 470.588 into exactly **one** prefill replica; HEAD prunes it and grants none. The per-cycle delta
is one prefill replica.

**One inference, flagged as an inference.** Because unpriced variants contribute no supply, base's
per-role RC does not decay as prefill grows, so base should add +1 to prefill *every* cycle while the
analyzer stays dead — a slow creep bounded only by the GPU limiter rather than a one-off. That follows
from the formula (`engine_v2.go:476-513`), but I did **not** run a multi-cycle fixture, so it is
reasoning, not measurement. Either way base is differently wrong, not right, and none of this is an
argument to revert `VG-up`.

## 4. What I am not claiming

Not measured and not asserted: what llm-d actually serves with prefill at 0 (a router/deployment
question); the *rate* at which real clusters enter this state; whether a synthesized floor is the
right repair (§3 of my last handoff — planner's and Dean's). `reclaimRole` remains un-instrumented
per-function; Finding 72 §2(i) bounds what that costs without closing it.

